,0.4,5],"m":"dark"},{"box":[40,-44,22.8,10,8,1.6],"m":"glass"},{"tree":[34,30,10,3.2]},{"tree":[34,50,10,3.2]},{"tree":[-60,-40,10,3.2]},{"tree":[-70,-40,10,3.2]}],"notes":"Kunstmuseum Basel: the Hauptbau (Rudolf Christ and Paul Bonatz, 1936) and, across Dufourstrasse, the Neubau (Christ & Gantenbein, 2016). Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the OpenStreetMap outlines in the ground file, not photographs. The ground shows the Hauptbau as a ring round its courtyard between St. Alban-Graben (north) and Dufourstrasse (east), with the museum's point on its east wing, and a lower range running on south; the five-storey block east of Dufourstrasse is the Neubau. So the Hauptbau was moved east onto its outline (about 56 by 69 m, courtyard about 24 by 37 m), the rear range added, the Neubau set on its own outline (about 40 by 56 m), and both streets laid where the ground has them. The Hauptbau: pale limestone, the arcade on the St. Alban-Graben front opening into the courtyard (Rodin's Burghers of Calais a dark group there), a string course, two upper storeys of tall narrow windows at a measured rhythm, cornice and attic, glass lanterns over the top-lit galleries. The Neubau: a near-windowless grey-brick monolith on a plinth, the dark frieze band near the top where its LED letters run, the recessed glazed entrance facing the Hauptbau, two slot windows. Still guessed: heights (Hauptbau about 21 m, Neubau 22 m), window and arch counts, the rear range's height, the lanterns' layout, and whether the rear range belongs to the museum."}